---
import OrcaDocs from "../../../../components/OrcaDocs.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import { ORCA_REPO } from "../../../../data/orca";

const DOCS = `${ORCA_REPO}/blob/main/docs`;
const HETZNER = `${DOCS}/hetzner.md`;

const description =
  "Upgrading Orca: releases and the images built from them, deploying a new image with its upgrade steps and the check after it, additive migrations so an older image runs on a newer schema, rollbacks without restoring the database, floor-raising migrations, backups and restores, two schedulers overlapping during a deploy, and keeping workers on the server's tag.";
---

<OrcaDocs href="/s/orca/docs/upgrades" description={description}>
  <p class="eyebrow">Operations</p>
  <h1 class="section-title">Upgrades and rollbacks</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    An upgrade is a new image. Orca keeps the database working for the image before it,
    so going back is another deploy rather than a restore. This page covers releases,
    the deploy, the schema rules that make rollbacks cheap, backups, and the workers.
  </p>

  <nav class="docs-toc" aria-label="On this page">
    <a href="#releases">Releases and images</a>
    <a href="#deploying">Deploying a new image</a>
    <a href="#migrations">Additive migrations</a>
    <a href="#rolling-back">Rolling back</a>
    <a href="#backups">Backups</a>
    <a href="#overlap">Overlapping schedulers</a>
    <a href="#workers">Workers on the same tag</a>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="releases">Releases and images</h2>
  <p>
    Each Orca release has a version, the <code>orca-mojo</code> version a project pins,
    and an entry in
    <a href={`${ORCA_REPO}/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md`} rel="noopener">CHANGELOG.md</a>. With
    it come:
  </p>
  <ul class="docs-list">
    <li>
      <code>ghcr.io/magmalake/orca-runtime:&lt;version&gt;</code> (from 0.24.0), for
      linux/arm64 and linux/amd64, with <code>orca</code>, its tools and the <code>/opt/orca</code>
      layout;
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>orca-hetzner.tar.gz</code>, the deploy scripts, which print the version they
      came with (<a href="/s/orca/docs/deploy#scripts">The scripts</a>).
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A project's image builds <code>FROM</code> the runtime of the Orca version it pins
    and adds its own pipelines. Images are tagged by the project's short commit sha, not
    by version, so moving a project to a new Orca is a commit that bumps the pin, and its
    image is the thing you deploy
    (<a href={`${HETZNER}#images`} rel="noopener">Images</a>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="deploying">Deploying a new image</h2>
  <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`./deploy.sh a1b2c3d                    # the image at that tag; starts again what changed
./deploy.sh --rollback                 # back to the tags deployed before`} />
  <p>
    <a href="/s/orca/docs/deploy#deploys">Deploying</a> has what a deploy replaces and
    how the UI and apps change without downtime. Three parts matter for an upgrade:
  </p>
  <ul class="docs-list">
    <li>
      <strong>A backup first.</strong> Each deploy that replaces anything backs the
      database up just before it applies, and prints the dump's key (since 0.16.0).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Upgrade steps</strong> (since 0.23.0). A release that needs something done
      around its deploy, such as a backfill after it or a new secret before it, lists it
      in its image's <code>/opt/orca/upgrade.toml</code>. When the Orca tag changes,
      <code>deploy.sh</code> reads the new image's <code>before</code> steps first and
      stops until each is done, then runs the <code>after</code> steps once the pipelines
      are registered and before they are unpaused. <code>orca upgrade --from &lt;old&gt;
      --to &lt;new&gt;</code> prints the steps between two versions, skipped versions'
      included.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The check after it.</strong> Every deploy, a rollback too, ends with
      <code>orca-stack check</code>: Postgres answers, each container runs the image it
      should, there is one scheduler, every pipeline's binary is in the image, and, on a
      dev stack, <code>CANARY</code> runs succeed. It undoes nothing; a failed check means
      <code>--rollback</code> is the way back
      (<a href={`${HETZNER}#the-check-after-each-deploy`} rel="noopener">The check after
      each deploy</a>).
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="migrations">Additive migrations</h2>
  <p>
    The new image migrates the schema when its scheduler or UI starts. Postgres is never
    rolled back with the image, so the older image has to run on the newer schema. It can,
    as long as every migration it doesn't know is additive. Orca's own rule for its
    migrations (<a href={`${ORCA_REPO}/blob/main/AGENTS.md`} rel="noopener">AGENTS.md</a>,
    "Migrations"):
  </p>
  <ul class="docs-list">
    <li>add columns only nullable or with a default;</li>
    <li>
      add tables and indexes freely; a foreign key onto a table Orca deletes from needs
      <code>ON DELETE CASCADE</code> or <code>SET NULL</code>;
    </li>
    <li>
      don't rename, drop or retype anything, tighten a constraint, change a key an
      <code>ON CONFLICT</code> names, write a status older code acts on, or give an old
      column a new meaning.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Queries name their columns and read rows by name, so a new column never shifts what
    older code reads.
  </p>

  <h3 id="schema-ahead">An older binary on a newer schema</h3>
  <p>
    When a binary starts on a database ahead of it, it checks the schema's floor: the
    fewest migrations a binary must know to run on it, which is the newest migration
    listed in <code>_floor_raisers</code> in <code>src/orca/migrations.mojo</code>. If
    the binary knows at least that many, it logs that it is running on a newer schema and
    goes on:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock label="log" code={`orca: the database is at schema version 41, ahead of this binary's 40; its migrations since are additive, so running on it`} />
  <p>
    If not, it refuses to start, naming the version it needs. The floor raisers so far
    are migration 1, which creates the schema, and 24, the EC2 warm pool's change to
    <code>orca.instances</code>' key. This holds from Orca 0.16.0, the first that
    records floors: an image with an older Orca stops at any newer schema, additive or
    not.
  </p>

  <h3 id="floor-raisers">Floor-raising migrations</h3>
  <p>
    A migration that has to break the rule adds its version to
    <code>_floor_raisers</code>, saying why. From then on, a rollback to an image from before it needs the database restored from the
    backup taken before the upgrade, and a deploy across it can't
    <a href="#overlap">overlap</a> schedulers.
  </p>

  <h2 id="rolling-back">Rolling back</h2>
  <p>
    Each deploy keeps the tags it replaced. <code>./deploy.sh --rollback</code> applies
    the previous Orca and app tags and, if it replaced the scheduler, registers the
    pipelines again from the older image, so runs start with the code that is deployed.
    A second rollback goes forward again. Rolling back only an app leaves the scheduler
    alone (<a href="/s/orca/docs/deploy#rollbacks">Rollbacks</a>).
  </p>
  <p>
    Across a floor-raising migration, restore the backup, then roll back:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`ssh root@<ip> orca-stack list-backups      # the dumps, by time
ssh root@<ip> orca-stack restore --force backups/pg/20261108T101500Z.dump
ssh root@<ip> orca-stack rollback --force  # the old images on it, registered again`} />
  <p>
    The restore unpauses the pipelines the deploy paused and leaves those paused by hand
    or by the watchdog paused. What happened in Orca's database since the dump is lost:
    runs, registrations, users and tokens added. The warehouse isn't in the dump, so
    snapshots committed since stay in the tables
    (<a href={`${HETZNER}#rolling-back`} rel="noopener">Rolling back</a>).
  </p>
  <p>
    A run with steps done by the newer code fails after a rollback, saying how to resume
    it with <code>--allow-new-code</code>. And register without <code>"auto"</code>
    requests before rolling back to an Orca before 0.21.0, which refuses them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="backups">Backups</h2>
  <p>
    The Hetzner server runs <code>orca-stack backup</code> every night at 02:30 UTC: a
    <code>pg_dump</code> of the <code>orca</code> schema, checked to read back, uploaded to
    <code>s3://&lt;bucket&gt;/backups/pg/&lt;time&gt;.dump</code>, keeping the newest 14.
    Beside each dump is a file saying which pipelines were paused by hand and which by a
    deploy. A failed backup sends an alert.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`./backup.sh                               # now, e.g. before a risky step
./backup.sh list
./up.sh --restore latest                  # onto a new server and an empty Volume`} />
  <p>
    Only Orca's own state (runs, pipelines, users) is in the dump. The warehouse is in the
    bucket and needs no restore. Test a restore before you need one
    (<a href={`${HETZNER}#backups-and-restore`} rel="noopener">Backups and restore</a>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="overlap">Overlapping schedulers</h2>
  <p>
    Restarting the scheduler kills the local runs it holds, so by default a deploy that
    replaces it pauses the pipelines and waits for those runs. With
    <code>orca-stack configure OVERLAP=1</code> (since 0.18.0, off by default) it
    doesn't. The new scheduler starts beside the old one on the same Postgres:
  </p>
  <ol class="docs-list">
    <li>
      <code>orca scheduler can-overlap</code>, from the new image, says yes only if a live
      scheduler leads and every live one knows at least the new binary's floor. On no,
      the deploy pauses and waits as before, saying why.
    </li>
    <li>
      The new scheduler migrates (additively) and joins. The newest live scheduler leads,
      by a Postgres advisory lock, so the old one gives up the lead and drains: it starts
      nothing more and finishes its runs under its own image.
    </li>
    <li>
      The deploy waits for the new one to lead (<code>orca scheduler wait-lead</code>),
      then registers the pipelines, so new runs start with the new code.
    </li>
    <li>
      The old scheduler exits once its runs end, or after
      <code>ORCA_DRAIN_SECONDS</code> (3600) kills what is left; those runs are queued
      again.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    A draining scheduler never takes the lead back. If the new one dies first, nothing
    starts until a newer one runs: the container's restart, or a rollback
    (<a href={`${DOCS}/overlap.md`} rel="noopener">overlap.md</a>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="workers">Workers on the same tag</h2>
  <p>
    A remote worker runs the code of its own image, so keep it on the server's tag.
    Deploy the server first, then restart the worker on the same tag. A server deploy
    doesn't wait for a worker's runs, and they go on to the new UI
    (<a href="/s/orca/docs/workers#limits">Remote workers</a>).
  </p>
  <p>
    If the pipelines are registered with <code>--image</code>, a worker of another image
    is given none of their runs, which stay queued saying why, so nothing runs on a
    mismatched worker. Without <code>--image</code>, a worker still on the old tag runs
    the old code until it is restarted. Machines of an
    <a href="/s/orca/docs/pools#autoscaling">autoscaled pool</a> run the image the UI
    gives them when they boot (<code>ORCA_WORKER_IMAGE</code>, the server's own), so
    machines made after a deploy get the new one; a machine already up keeps the image it
    booted with until it is removed.
  </p>
</OrcaDocs>
